      "## Off-the-shelf AI is good enough for many jobs",
      "Before talking about custom models, it is worth saying plainly: for many business tasks, a well-configured off-the-shelf model is the right choice. Leading general-purpose models are capable at drafting, summarising, answering questions and handling conversations. Paired with your own documents through retrieval, they can answer accurately from your information without any training at all.",
      "A private, fine-tuned model is a bigger investment. It makes sense when there is a specific problem it solves better than the alternatives. This post sets out how to tell the difference.",
      "## The options, from simplest to most custom",
      "- Prompting: a general model with clear instructions and examples. Fast to set up, flexible, good for drafting and general tasks.",
      "- Retrieval, often called RAG: the model searches your documents and answers from them, ideally citing its sources. Good for knowledge that changes often, such as policies, prices or procedures.",
      "- Fine-tuning: training a model further on examples of your work, so it learns your style, structure, terminology and the way you make decisions.",
      "- Private deployment: running a model, fine-tuned or not, in an environment you control, such as your own cloud account or your own hardware, so your data never leaves it.",
      "These can be combined. Many strong setups use a privately deployed model with retrieval for facts and light fine-tuning for format and tone.",
      "## When fine-tuning makes sense",
      "- You have a repeated task with a consistent structure: drafting a certain type of letter, classifying enquiries, extracting fields from documents or writing reports in a fixed format.",
      "- You have plenty of good examples: hundreds or thousands of past outputs that reflect how you want the work done.",
      "- Prompting and retrieval get close but not consistent: the output drifts in format or misses specialist conventions.",
      "- Volume is high: a smaller fine-tuned model can be cheaper and faster to run than a large general model for the same narrow task.",
      "- The knowledge is specialist: niche terminology or reasoning that general models handle poorly.",
      "Fine-tuning is less useful for keeping facts up to date. If prices, rules or procedures change, retrieval is usually the better tool, because you update a document rather than retrain a model.",
      "## When private deployment makes sense",
      "- Data rules: you handle health records, legal files, financial data or other sensitive information, and your clients, regulators or insurers expect it to stay within your control.",
      "- Confidentiality: your knowledge is your competitive advantage, and you do not want it processed by third-party services.",
      "- Data residency: you need processing to stay in the UK.",
      "- Predictable costs: high, steady usage can make a dedicated deployment more economical than paying per request.",
      "- Control: you want to decide when the model changes, rather than having a provider update it underneath you and alter how it behaves.",
      "Business plans from the major AI providers do offer meaningful protections, such as not training on your data and limiting retention. For many firms, that is enough. Private deployment is for when it is not.",
      "## Questions to ask before you invest",
      "- What exact task should the model do, and how will we judge whether it does it well?",
      "- Have we tried a strong general model with good prompts and retrieval? Where exactly does it fall short?",
      "- Do we have enough clean, representative examples, and do we have the right to use them for training?",
      "- What data will the model see, and what do our regulators, clients and insurers require?",
      "- Who will maintain it, monitor its quality and retrain it when needed?",
      "- What does it cost to run, compared with the time it saves or the revenue it supports?",
      "If the answers are unclear, start with the simpler option and collect data as you go. Real usage, with people correcting and approving outputs, often produces the training examples you need later. Nothing is wasted by starting small.",
      "## What a private AI project involves",
      "### Discovery and evaluation",
      "Define the task, gather examples and build a test set that reflects real work, including the difficult cases. Benchmark general models against it to set a baseline. Sometimes this step shows that a general model is already good enough, which is a useful answer in itself.",
      "### Data preparation",
      "Clean the examples, remove or anonymise personal data where appropriate, and put them in a consistent format. This is often the largest part of the work, and the part that most affects the result.",
      "### Training and testing",
      "Fine-tune an open-weight model on your data and compare it with the baseline on the test set. Have your specialists review outputs side by side, not just automated scores, because they will spot the errors that matter.",
      "### Deployment and monitoring",
      "Deploy in your chosen environment with access controls, logging and a clear route for people to flag poor answers. Monitor quality over time, collect feedback and plan periodic retraining as your work evolves.",
      "Our approach to private AI is simple: start with the job, measure it, and only add complexity when it earns its place.",
