      "## Check your suppliers properly",
      "Your AI provider will usually act as a processor on your instructions. You remain the controller and stay responsible. Before you sign, check:",
      "- That there is a data processing agreement meeting UK GDPR requirements.",
      "- Where data is stored and processed. If it leaves the UK, which transfer mechanism applies, such as a UK adequacy decision, the International Data Transfer Agreement or the UK addendum to the EU standard contractual clauses.",
      "- Whether your data is used to train the provider's models, and whether that can be switched off.",
      "- Retention settings for logs, transcripts and recordings.",
      "- Security measures, access controls and any independent certifications.",
      "- The list of sub-processors, and how you will be told about changes.",
      "Many business-grade AI services offer settings that exclude your data from training and limit retention. Consumer versions of the same tools often do not. Make sure your team is using the right one, and that nobody is pasting customer details into a personal account.",
      "## Run an impact assessment where needed",
      "A data protection impact assessment is required when processing is likely to result in a high risk to individuals. The ICO's guidance points to factors such as innovative technology and large-scale processing of special category data. If you are a clinic putting an AI receptionist on your phones, assume you need one. Even where it is not strictly required, a short assessment is a useful discipline. It should cover:",
      "- What the system does and why you need it.",
      "- Whether it is necessary and proportionate, or whether a less intrusive option would work.",
      "- The risks to individuals, such as inaccurate answers, data leaks or keeping data too long.",
      "- The measures that reduce those risks, and who is responsible for them.",
      "## Design the chatbot to minimise risk",
      "Good design does most of the compliance work for you.",
      "- Collect only what you need for the next step. A booking needs a name, contact details and a reason, not a full history.",
      "- Do not let the chatbot make decisions with legal or similarly significant effects on people without meaningful human involvement. Use it to gather, answer and route.",
      "- Ground its answers in your approved information, so it does not invent policies, prices or promises.",
      "- Avoid storing sensitive details in logs where you can, or redact them automatically.",
      "- Set retention periods and delete transcripts automatically when they expire.",
      "- Restrict which members of your team can view conversations.",
      "- Make sure your process for subject access and erasure requests covers chatbot and call data.",
      "- Test it with awkward questions, including attempts to get it to reveal other people's information.",
      "### When private AI is the better route",
      "For some firms, the safest option is a model deployed privately, either in your own cloud account or with a provider that hosts it in the UK and never shares the data. That is particularly relevant if you handle large volumes of sensitive information, or want an assistant that draws on confidential internal knowledge. Our Private AI work exists for exactly these situations: language models fine-tuned on your data and deployed in an environment you control.",
      "## Getting it right from the start",
      "Compliance is far easier to build in than to bolt on. When we design AI receptionists and chatbots, data mapping, supplier checks, disclosure and retention are part of the build, not an afterthought. If you want to use AI with customer data and are not sure where you stand, our AI Growth Audit includes a review of your lead handling and the data it touches. Book an AI Growth Audit from our contact page, or get in touch to talk it through.",
