      "## Why legal Google Ads go wrong",
      "Legal search terms are among the most competitive in paid search. Clicks can be expensive, and a poorly set-up account can spend a month's budget on people looking for free advice, legal jobs or a firm in another city. The firms that do well treat Google Ads as a system: tight targeting, landing pages that answer the question, fast follow-up and tracking that shows which clicks become instructed clients.",
      "## Structure campaigns around practice areas and intent",
      "Start by separating campaigns by practice area. Family, conveyancing, employment, personal injury, criminal defence, and wills and probate all have different fee levels, margins and search behaviour. Mixing them in one campaign makes it impossible to see what is working or to set sensible budgets.",
      "Within each practice area, group keywords by intent:",
      "- Urgent need: searches such as police station solicitor or arrested need a solicitor. These suit call-focused ads and out-of-hours cover.",
      "- Ready to instruct: divorce solicitor near me, conveyancing quote, unfair dismissal lawyer.",
      "- Researching: how long does probate take, or can I contest a will. These are usually cheaper but less likely to convert straight away, so they often suit content and remarketing rather than heavy bidding.",
      "Location matters. Set targeting to people in or regularly in your area rather than people showing interest in it, unless you deliberately take national work. For firms with several offices, consider a campaign per office with its own ads and landing pages.",
      "Ad schedule matters too. If nobody answers the phone after six, either stop call-only ads then or make sure someone, or something, does answer.",
      "## Keywords and negatives",
      "Use phrase and exact match for your core terms while you learn what converts. Broad match can work well once good conversion data is feeding smart bidding, but without that data it tends to spend on loosely related searches.",
